TEXT -936 -200 Left 2 !.tran 0 5 0 0.1m
TEXT -936 -208 Left 2 !.param tdv=1 tdt=1 fn1=50 omega_n={2*pi*fn1} ce=5012n ce1=10n p=1.09
TEXT -936 -224 Left 2 !.param last=20e-6
TEXT -352 0 Left 4 ;test also\nt_Start=0.200\nand\nt_Start=0.205
TEXT -936 -192 Left 2 !.options vntol=1m abstol=1u\n.options plotwinsize=0 numdgt=10; Für FFT
TEXT -936 -216 Left 2 ;.param last=20e6
TEXT -744 88 Left 4 ;See that the pet current flows samephased to \nthe transformer \nIphase = Ipet / 3
TEXT -792 -168 Left 4 ;The current at the fault position is at startup due to capacitive charging currents\nvery high and goes to zero at the end of the simulation\nThe frequency of the charging currents is also very high
TEXT -1392 -200 Left 4 ;The voltage source is loaded only at the transient process\nof the earth fault case (high frequency cap. charging currents)\nAt the end of the transient process = steady state= the current is \nas high as at begin since the additional charging currents are\nnow compensated by the petersen.
TEXT -1192 360 Left 5 ;Smaller net with 300A ( instead of  900A Petersen sum above )
TEXT -312 312 Left 4 ;See:\n \nAt earth fault the kap. reactive power of the net is suddenly doubled. \nThis is why now two phases has a phase/phase level voltage\nand so the reactive power is 2 times the value before fault. This additional\nreacitve power burden the voltage source only for a short time.\nAt the time rises this additonal power is compensated by the petersen\nand so the same reactive power is reached as at startup
TEXT -104 -208 Left 4 ;The voltage source is loaded only at the transient process\nof the earth fault case (high frequency cap. charging currents)\nAt the end of the transient process = steady state= the current is \nas high as at begin since the additional charging currents are\nnow compensated by the petersen.
TEXT -672 -232 Left 5 ;Big net with 900A Petersen sum
TEXT -312 464 Left 4 ;NOTE: \nIn this example the inductances (impedance model) of the line are\nnot assumed coupled. Due to coupling the impedance risis \n(about two times) in the faulty phase. Therefore the short circuit\ncurrent will be smaller.
